  "body": {
    "id": "thm:ordinary-id-reduction",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "If \\(H=\\{s_{\\emptyset}\\}\\), every selector gate is laid back on \\(E_H\\), and \\(S\\) has no latent root shared with \\(V\\), then \\(G_H^N\\) is the ordinary semi-Markovian expansion on \\(V\\), \\(\\Phi_H\\) equals its ordinary observational-target relation, and \\(\\operatorname{SA\\mbox{-}CS\\mbox{-}ID}\\) reduces to the ID formula-or-hedge classification of Shpitser and Pearl.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "H",
      "sempty",
      "EH",
      "S",
      "V",
      "GN",
      "PhiH",
      "SACSID"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:exact-selector-support",
      "ass:known-selector-gates",
      "ass:latent-root-independence",
      "thm:normalization-equivalence",
      "lem:computed-closure-valid-sequence",
      "prop:input-determinacy",
      "lem:ordinary-id-formula-or-hedge",
      "def:sa-csid-machine",
      "def:fixing-transition",
      "def:elimination-transition"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Exact support on the singleton gives\n\\[\n p_\\theta(S=s_{\\emptyset})=1.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nEvery selector multiplexer is laid back there, so it selects its natural response table.  Because \\(S\\) shares no latent root with \\(V\\), suppressing the constant selector deletes neither a substantive response coordinate nor an independence restriction among substantive roots.  Support-active normalization therefore yields exactly the ordinary semi-Markovian expansion on \\(V\\).  Moreover,\n\\[\n p_\\theta(V=v,S=s_{\\emptyset})=p_\\theta(V=v),\n\\]\nand setting \\(S=s_{\\emptyset}\\) in the target changes no substantive equation.  Normalization equivalence then identifies \\(\\Phi_H\\), after suppressing the constant coordinate, with the ordinary observational--interventional relation.\n\nNow specialize the deterministic case function.  The only context is laid back, \\(\\operatorname{seriousSet}=\\varnothing\\), and the context graph is the ordinary ADMG.  Ancestor restriction and district spawning are therefore the ordinary ID cases.  The closure enumerator computes the unique ordinary reachable closure, and \\(\\operatorname{fixSeq}\\) supplies a valid order for precisely its complement.  If \\(C=D^{*}\\), computed fixing emits the ordinary reachable district factor.  If \\(C\\ne D^{*}\\), separation of \\(S\\) from \\(V\\) puts the task in the \\(S\\notin C\\) branch; success of \\(\\operatorname{fixSeq}_{\\widetilde G}(C\\setminus D^{*})\\) emits the ordinary recursive factor, while failure records the same thicket/hedge pair \\((D^{*},C)\\) as the ordinary recursion.  Truncated factorization never enters as selector hybridization because there is no serious selector child.\n\nThus, by induction on the ordinary recursion tree, every case, computed graph, fixed set, kernel quotient, and terminal success or hedge is identical to the Shpitser--Pearl ID case.  The cited ordinary ID classification identifies those endpoints as formula or hedge.  This is a structural specialization of \\(\\operatorname{next}_I\\); it neither assumes inclusion in a residual candidate list nor uses the unresolved fixed-support residual dichotomy.",
    "justification": "Singleton laid-back support suppresses the separated selector and makes every case-function branch, computed closure, fixed set, and endpoint identical to ordinary ID.",
    "gap": "The earlier proof incorrectly made this structural sanity check depend on general fixed-support residual candidate exhaustiveness.",
    "consumer": "Implementers can compare ordinary inputs directly with classical ID formula or hedge outputs without invoking the open fixed-support residual theorem.",
